From 8a97521d7e2d0ee343817e2acde1b50b5d0c578b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daedalao <daedalao@hotmail.com>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 12:08:57 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 22/22] mozglue: dispatch the PPC64 VSX string search at
 runtime

The VSX memchr16/32/64 and memchr2x8/2x16 kernels in SIMD.cpp were gated
on __VSX__ at compile time. A big-endian ppc64 build with the
distribution's baseline flags (-mcpu=powerpc64 -maltivec) therefore never
got them, and a build that enabled VSX globally to get them would no
longer run on CPUs without VSX such as the PowerPC 970.

Move the kernels unchanged into SIMD_vsx.cpp, built with -mcpu=power8
-mvsx the same way SIMD_avx2.cpp is built with -mavx2, and call them from
SIMD.cpp only when AT_HWCAP/AT_HWCAP2 report VSX and ISA 2.07. The check
is done once and cached; when SIMD.cpp itself is built for POWER8 (the
little-endian baseline) it folds to a constant. MOZ_PPC64_FORCE_POWER4=1
forces the scalar code. SIMD_vsx.cpp includes no Mozilla or C++ library
headers so that no POWER8-compiled inline or template definition can be
picked by the linker for callers elsewhere in mozglue.

mozglue/misc is also linked into the standalone JS shell, which does not
have the mozilla/ppc.h runtime (mozglue/build/ppc.cpp), so the check is
done locally with getauxval().

Tested on POWER8 (ppc64le) with gcc 16 and clang 22, SIMD.cpp built for
POWER7 to force the dispatch path: 800k randomized trials against a naive
reference plus a page-boundary over-read check, with and without
MOZ_PPC64_FORCE_POWER4=1. memchr16 over 64K char16_t without a match:
5.6 us dispatched to VSX, 45.5 us with the override.
